# .aiignore -AAHP Safety Firewall (AGENT-FACING, NOT ENFORCED)
# Patterns that must NEVER appear in handoff files.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE YOU RELY ON IT.
# No gate parses this file. Not `aahp lint`, not `aahp verify`, not `aahp check`,
# not any shipped CI workflow. The header here used to assert that CI hooks
# validate these patterns. That was false, and a pattern added below has
# never been checked by anything. This file is a briefing for the agents that
# write your handoff files, and it is only as good as their compliance.
#
# What IS enforced, and where the list actually lives:
#   secrets    scripts/lint-handoff.sh, SECRET_PATTERNS (a fixed array)
#   injection  scripts/lint-handoff.sh, check 1 (a fixed array)
#   PII        scripts/lint-handoff.sh, check 3, plus .ai/handoff/pii-allowlist.json
# Editing this file does not change any of those. `aahp lint` prints how many
# patterns you have here that it is not applying.
#
# Do NOT put a secret, an internal hostname or an address range in a handoff file
# on the strength of this file existing.

# ============================================
# Secrets & API Keys
# ============================================
*_KEY=*
*_SECRET=*
*_TOKEN=*
*_PASSWORD=*
*_CREDENTIALS=*
Bearer *
sk-*
ghp_*
gho_*
glpat-*
xoxb-*
xoxp-*
AKIA*
-----BEGIN*PRIVATE KEY-----
-----BEGIN*RSA*-----

# ============================================
# PII (Personally Identifiable Information)
# ============================================
# Email patterns
*@*.com
*@*.de
*@*.org
*@*.net
*@*.io

# SSN pattern (US)
# \b\d{3}-\d{2}-\d{4}\b

# Phone numbers (international)
# \+\d{1,3}[\s-]?\d{3,14}

# ============================================
# Prompt Injection Patterns
# ============================================
ignore all previous*
ignore prior*
disregard*instructions*
you are now*
new system prompt*
override*safety*
act as*unrestricted*
jailbreak*

# ============================================
# Internal Infrastructure
# ============================================
# There is no enforcement behind this section, so it is not a place to put an
# internal hostname and stop worrying about it. Adding "10.0.0.*" here does not
# make anything reject "10.0.0.5" in STATUS.md; that was measured and it is why
# the invitation that used to stand here was removed.
#
# To keep internal infrastructure out of committed handoff files, do one of:
#   - do not write it into the handoff files in the first place (the only
#     control that works today);
#   - add a `forbiddenPatterns` rule in aahp.config.json with an `include` of
#     `.ai/handoff/*.md`. That IS enforced, by `aahp check`, and it fails the
#     build. Note the rule text itself lands in a tracked file, so express a
#     hostname as a pattern rather than as the literal string you are hiding.
#   - add the pattern to SECRET_PATTERNS in your vendored scripts/lint-handoff.sh
#     if you carry one.
